  "summary": "Six preprints from 12–17 November 2026 span quantum-hardware subspace methods and classical wavefunction closures for strong correlation.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is restricting strongly correlated problems to tractable subspaces or compact parameterizations. On the quantum-computing side, a machine-learned active-space selector feeds sample-based quantum diagonalization on IBM hardware for transition-metal spin gaps; a boson representation aims to reduce quantum Krylov/QSE circuit depth; and Stabilizer-Subspace Perturbation Theory recovers correlations lost in stabilizer projection. Classical counterparts include a cumulant-kernel closure in a Quantum Boltzmann Equation for static correlation, transfer-matrix calculus for infinite MPS with exact long-range interactions, and compact projected Pfaffian states for Rydberg-blockaded PXP systems. The new element is mainly the pairing of subspace truncation with explicit correction or closure, tested on molecular benchmarks (H3, H4, BeH2, H2O, N2, benzyne, hydrogen chains) and lattice models.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
